  "memories" : [
    {
      "content" : "# MyFIRE\n\n**Purpose:** Personal FIRE (Financial Independence, Retire Early) planning app.\n\n## Components\n- **tracker-2 — 👤 My FIRE Profile:** One-row tracker holding the user's personal inputs (current_age, target_age, income, expenses, portfolio, monthly_contrib).\n- **calculator-1 — 🔥 FIRE Calculator:** Main calculator. Pulls live values from the Profile via linkedField rows. Outputs core FIRE stats, savings rate, years to FIRE, and all 5 variants (Lean, Regular, Fat, Coast, Barista).\n\n## Key rules\n- Always activate the relevant skill (`get_skill_tracker` or `get_skill_calculator`) before using its tools.\n- Profile edits flow automatically into calculator outputs — no manual sync needed.\n- Use `linkItem` \/ `unlinkItem` to connect tracker rows to calculator rows when adding new linked fields.\n",
      "path" : "AGENTS.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "# MyFIRE\n\n**Type:** tracker\n\n## Instructions\nCanvas hosts COMPONENTS (sub-canvases, ids like \"tracker-1\") — pass `componentId` to target one; single-component myApps may omit it. Per-kind tools unlock only after `addComponent` introduces that kind.\n\nLINKING — linkItem \/ unlinkItem attach refs between any two items (any kinds, any direction, same-component allowed for parent \/ subtask).",
      "path" : "pupa\/AGENTS.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Skills, subagents and multi-agent slack rooms\nwhen_to_use: when creating skills or subagents, using \/commands, or working with slack rooms\nversion: 7\n---\n- **Skills** — reusable playbooks that double as `\/commands` in chat.\n  Each app has its own set (this guide is one). Ask the agent to create\n  one, or write it yourself in the memory tree under `pupa\/skills\/`.\n- **Subagents** — named delegates with their own persona and\n  instructions. The main agent creates them and hands off focused tasks.\n- **Orchestrator** — the top-level agent: creates and manages myapps and\n  delegates work to each app's agent.\n\n**Slack rooms.** A component with channels, group DMs and DMs. The\nworkspace roster is simply *all* the myapp's subagents — creating a\nsubagent adds it to the roster; nothing lives in the component but rooms\nand their transcripts. @-mention an agent (or post in its DM) to wake it:\nit reads the channel's history for context, works with the app's tools,\nand posts its reply back to the channel. Agents can @-mention each other,\nso one message can start a chain of replies. Exporting the app keeps the\nchannels and the agent personas but strips the transcripts.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-agents\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: The component shapes a myapp canvas can hold and how they combine\nwhen_to_use: when choosing, combining or linking components, or asked what shapes exist\nversion: 7\n---\nA myapp's canvas holds components. Available shapes:\n\n- **calculator** — live numeric model with tunable inputs + formula rows\n- **calendar** — time-indexed events in a list or month grid\n- **chart** — pie\/bar\/line visualisation of tracker or calculator data\n- **checklist** — simple done\/not-done items, no per-row fields\n- **slack** — multi-agent chat rooms (subagent personas, channels, @-mentions)\n- **tracker** — multi-field records in a table\/kanban (fields, filters, cards)\n\n**Combining.** One myapp mixes shapes freely. Items link across\ncomponents — a tracker row to a calendar event, a task to a parent\nrecord. Calculators and charts read live from other components, and a\nchart can be embedded straight into chat.\n\n**Views, not shapes.** Kanban is a tracker view mode (table ⇄ cards),\nnot a separate component.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-components\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: How Pupa remembers: memories, sessions, change history, archive\nwhen_to_use: when asked what persists, how to undo changes, or how to hide an app\nversion: 7\n---\n- **Memories** — a filesystem-like tree of markdown notes and folders\n  that lives *inside* the app, not on the device's file system. Browse\n  and edit it from the Memories tab; the agent reads and writes it too.\n  Persists across sessions.\n- **Sessions** — \"New session\" starts a fresh conversation. The canvas\n  and memories stay.\n- **History** — every canvas change is recorded per myapp. Browse the\n  History tab and restore any earlier state in one tap. Tap **Take\n  snapshot** to pin the current state permanently (kept forever); pinned\n  snapshots can be **Export**ed as a `.pupa` file. Pins survive deleting\n  the myapp — find them all in **Settings ▸ Pinned snapshots**, where a\n  deleted app can be restored (revived) from a pin.\n- **Archive** — hide a myapp without deleting it (its data and memories\n  are kept). Browse, restore or delete from Settings ▸ Archive.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-memory\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Share and install myapps as .pupa files\nwhen_to_use: when exporting, sharing, importing or installing an app\nversion: 7\n---\nA `.pupa` file is an inert snapshot of a myapp: its components, optionally\nyour records and memories, plus the app's agent instructions and skills.\nNo code runs from the file itself.\n\n**Export.** Settings ▸ Import & Export: pick components, choose whether\nrecords and memories ride along, then Share (AirDrop, Messages, Files…).\n\n**Install.** Open a `.pupa` file (or import from Settings). A confirmation\nsheet shows what's inside before anything is added. Imported agent\ninstructions run with your tools once installed — only install bundles\nyou trust.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-sharing\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Where things live: the on-device app vs the backend, and standing instructions\nwhen_to_use: when unsure whether something belongs in the app or the backend, which skill system you're touching, or how to change standing behaviour\nversion: 7\n---\n**App vs backend.** Everything the user sees — components and their\nitems, memories, skills, `AGENTS.md` — is app state, created and edited\n**on the user's device** through the app's own tools. The backend only\nruns the model plus a few server-side tools (web search, shell, …) and\nstores **no app data**. Never write app content to a backend path or via\na shell unless explicitly told to; if unsure where something belongs, ask.\n\n**Canvas vs memory.** Components hold data items; memory is a *separate*\nper-app markdown note tree for durable context — not canvas data. Two\ndifferent stores; don't cross them. Memory paths are an in-app structure,\nnot host file paths — never reach for it via a shell.\n\n**Two skill systems.** *App* skills are on-device `SKILL.md` files —\nyours under `pupa\/skills\/<name>\/`, bundled ones under\n`pupa\/plugins\/<plugin>\/skills\/<name>\/` (this guide is one). A backend may\nseparately expose its own read-only skills library. App skills are\nalways the on-device files.\n\n**Standing behaviour.** Persistent instructions for an app's main agent\nlive at `pupa\/AGENTS.md` (subagents: `pupa\/agents\/<name>\/AGENTS.md`).\nEdit those to change behaviour permanently — not memory, not a skill.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-system\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: What Pupa is and what it can do — guide entry point\nwhen_to_use: when the user asks what Pupa is, what it can do, or how its pieces fit together\nversion: 7\n---\nPupa builds apps around you: describe what you need in chat and the agent\nassembles a **myapp** — a canvas of components it edits live while you talk.\n\n**Boundaries — who is what**\n- **Orchestrator** — the top-level chat. Creates, renames, archives and\n  deletes myapps and delegates work to their agents. Has its own memories\n  and skills; no canvas of its own.\n- **Myapp** — one workspace: its own agent, canvas, memories, skills and\n  subagents.\n- **Component** — one shape on a myapp's canvas (tracker, calendar, …).\n- **Item** — one record inside a component (row, event, task…).\n- **Memories** — each myapp (and the orchestrator) keeps its own\n  filesystem-like tree of markdown notes and folders living *inside* the\n  app — not on the device's file system. Edited by you and the agent\n  alike; persists across sessions. Durable context, not records.\n\nItems can link across components *within one myapp*. Myapps don't share\ndata — an app moves between devices or people as a `.pupa` file.\n\n**Go deeper** (users: type the `\/command`; agents: `app_skill_view`)\n- \/pupa-components — the shapes a canvas can hold and how they combine\n- \/pupa-sharing — export and install myapps as `.pupa` files\n- \/pupa-memory — memories, sessions, change history, archive\n- \/pupa-agents — skills, subagents and slack rooms\n- \/pupa-system — where things live: on-device app vs backend, standing instructions",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Onboards a new user to MyFIRE — helps them fill in their first FIRE profile by editing the template row, and answers questions about FIRE and the inputs it needs.\nwhen_to_use: When the user is getting started, asks how to set up their profile, wants help entering their numbers, or asks what FIRE is \/ what a field means.\n---\n\n# MyFIRE Setup\n\nGoal: get the user's real numbers into **My FIRE Profile** (`tracker-2`) and connect them to the **FIRE Calculator** (`calculator-1`), while answering any FIRE questions along the way.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Find the template row.** Read `tracker-2` (`listTrackerItems`). There's usually a starter row named \"Current Profile\" with placeholder values. Reuse it — don't create a duplicate. If several profiles exist, ask which one is theirs.\n\n2. **Gather the six inputs.** Ask for whatever is missing, in plain language. Accept ranges\/estimates — this is refinable later. Fields:\n   - `current_age` — their age today.\n   - `target_age` — the age they'd like to reach FIRE \/ stop needing to work.\n   - `income` — annual take-home (after tax) income.\n   - `expenses` — annual living expenses (what they actually spend per year).\n   - `portfolio` — total invested\/liquid net worth today (savings + investments).\n   - `monthly_contrib` — how much they add to investments each month.\n\n3. **Write into the template row** with `patchTrackerItems` (use the row's `id`). Rename it to something personal if they like (e.g. \"My Plan\"). Don't wipe fields they didn't answer — leave prior values.\n\n4. **Point the calculator at it.** The calculator's LINKED_FIELD rows pull from a profile row. If it isn't already linked to their row, use `setCalcRowLink` so the outputs reflect their numbers.\n\n5. **Show the result.** Summarize their key outputs (years to FIRE, FIRE number, FIRE age) and offer to tweak assumptions.\n\n## Answering FIRE questions\n\nKeep answers short and concrete. Common ones:\n\n- **What is FIRE?** Financial Independence, Retire Early. Build enough invested assets that their returns cover your living expenses, so paid work becomes optional.\n- **What's the \"FIRE number\"?** The portfolio size where you're financially independent. Rule of thumb: annual expenses × 25 (the 4% safe-withdrawal rule).\n- **Savings rate** — the single biggest lever: `(income − expenses) \/ income`. Higher rate = far fewer years to FIRE.\n- **FIRE variants:** Lean (bare-bones expenses), Regular, Fat (generous lifestyle), Coast (already saved enough that growth alone gets you there — no more contributions needed), Barista (part-time income bridges the gap).\n- **Why after-tax income & real expenses?** The model compares what comes in vs what goes out; both must be like-for-like and realistic or the projection drifts.\n\nIf a field is genuinely unknown, suggest a sensible estimate and mark it as a guess to revisit.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/setup\/SKILL.md"
    }
  ]
}